<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thaj</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thaj is an archaeological site in the Eastern Province of Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, inland from the Gulf port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Jubail</a>. It was a large walled city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a>, from about the 4th century BCE to the early centuries CE, and has yielded inscriptions, coins and a rich girl's burial with gold jewellery. Some scholars identify it with ancient Gerrha.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thaj.jpg" alt="Thaj" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: S0lL0 TRAVELER · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:50_years_BC_civilization.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Thaj lies about 90 kilometres west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Jubail</a>, on the edge of a small village called Sabkha, and about 95 km from the coast of the Arabian Gulf. It is a rich oasis with ancient palm groves and more than twenty old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a>, twelve of which are still in use, among ruins of buildings of black <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudbrick">mud brick</a>. Surveys in the 1960s found many burial mounds to the west, south and east of the town, some ring-shaped and up to 50 metres across, others marked by capstones or stone cists.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Excavations since the mid-20th century have traced the town's long life. A Danish mission under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geoffrey-bibby">Geoffrey Bibby</a> dug a small area in 1968, finding Seleucid pottery and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> arrowheads. A joint Harvard-Saudi team in 1977 confirmed trade links in the Hellenistic, Parthian and Sassanian periods, and a Saudi-UK excavation of 1983 found graveyards, tombstones with Hasaitic inscriptions, clay animal figures and 36 coins, concluding that settlement may have run from 300 BCE to 300 CE. Work between 2000 and 2006 under Abdulhameed al-Hashash showed that salt from the surrounding marshes was exported.</p><p>Two inscribed amphoras from burials outside the city carry one text in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a>, probably taken over from Achaemenid administration, and one in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-arabia">South Arabian</a> cursive, or zabur, the first attestation of that script in the core region of Hasaitic writing. Both scripts were thus used side by side in Eastern Arabia, and the spread of the South Arabian one is thought to reflect trade between Gerrha and South Arabia around 300 BCE.</p><p>Thaj remained known after antiquity. A castle was built there in the late 6th century by Rashid ibn Shihab al-Yashkuri, and the Umayyad poets Dhu al-Rummah and al-Farazdaq named it. Arab geographers of the 10th and 11th centuries, al-Hamdani and al-Bakri, described it as a watering place and village of al-Bahrayn, and it served as a caravan stop. The first European to visit it was W. H. Shakespeare, who found two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-arabia">South Arabian</a> grave inscriptions, the first of their kind known in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Thāj” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its site has never been identified with certainty; <b>Thaj</b>, Hofuf and the coast near Uqair have all been proposed.</blockquote><a href="/state/gerrha">Gerrha</a></li></ul></section>
